<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hans Holbein the Younger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1497–1543</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hans Holbein the Younger was a German-born painter and printmaker who worked in Basel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and is regarded as one of the greatest portraitists of the sixteenth century. In Basel he painted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> and designed woodcuts, including The Dance of Death. He became court painter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII of England</a>, and his works include The Ambassadors (1533) and portraits of Thomas More and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cromwell">Thomas Cromwell</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hans-holbein-the-younger.jpg" alt="Hans Holbein the Younger" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Holbein the Younger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hans_Holbein_the_Younger,_self-portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
